1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,950
Dr. Eric Berg puts it in one breath: "what does organic mean it
means without pesticides herbicides fungicides and insecticides".

2
00:00:09,467 --> 00:00:17,042
So what are organic farmers actually allowed to spray? And if they
do spray, does eating organic still keep pesticides out of you?

3
00:00:18,067 --> 00:00:24,117
Dr. Mindy Pelz describes organic produce as "a
food that has technically not been sprayed".

4
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:27,871
The quickest way to check is
to read the rulebook itself.

5
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:45,787
The US organic regulations say that when a farm's preventive practices are not enough, a biological or botanical substance, or
a synthetic one from an approved national list, "may be applied to prevent, suppress, or control pests, weeds, or diseases".

6
00:00:45,800 --> 00:01:03,150
That list is headed "Synthetic substances allowed for use in organic crop production", so not even every synthetic pesticide is off
the table. Europe's organic list is no purer: it names paraffin oils, lime sulphur and copper compounds such as Bordeaux mixture.

7
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:09,542
So organic farms do spray. The sharper argument
is about whether what they spray is any gentler.

8
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:17,592
Kurzgesagt tells its viewers, "Organic pesticides
are not necessarily safer than conventional ones."

9
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:24,725
Simnett Nutrition takes the other side: "they're generally
not as harmful and they're more closely regulated".

10
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:39,746
A review of organic food and health went through Europe's approved list: of 385 substances authorised as pesticides
in the EU, 26 are also approved for organic farming, "evaluated in accordance with the same legal framework".

11
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:51,179
On the full list, 340 have some identified toxicity, meaning regulators
set a safe intake or exposure limit for them; on the organic list, 10 do.

12
00:01:51,500 --> 00:02:03,013
And 27 substances on the full list are classed as "Suspected human carcinogens"; on
the organic list, none are, and none is classed as a reproductive toxicant either.

13
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:13,233
Keep in mind what those tables measure: a substance's built-in hazard, not
how much of it reaches anyone, and only for Europe's list, not America's.

14
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:24,583
The review also names the exceptions. Pyrethrins, an extract of a chrysanthemum,
"share the same mechanism of action as the synthetic pyrethroid insecticides".

15
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:33,163
And copper, a nutrient we all need, is "toxic at high intakes"
and a concern for the creatures living in rivers and lakes.

16
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:44,192
Europe's organic rules even allow a pair of synthetic pyrethroids, but only inside
insect traps, where the review judges the risk of them reaching food negligible.

17
00:02:44,700 --> 00:03:02,225
So organic does not mean unsprayed. In America the rules allow listed sprays once prevention has failed; in Europe, where the hazard
tables come from, the organic list is mostly of low concern on those tables, with pyrethrins and copper as the real exceptions.

18
00:03:03,233 --> 00:03:11,071
Which leaves the question most shoppers really care about: if organic
farms spray too, does eating organic change what ends up inside you?

19
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:21,163
In a DW Documentary film, one interviewee goes further: "the primary
advantage of organic produce is that there are no pesticide residues".

20
00:03:21,167 --> 00:03:25,242
No residues at all? Food
testing says otherwise.

21
00:03:25,767 --> 00:03:38,592
Averaged over recent years of European food monitoring, measurable residues within legal
limits turned up in 43.7 percent of all samples, and in 13.8 percent of organic ones.

22
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:48,725
American monitoring data tell the same story from the other side: in the federal
Pesticide Data Program, 23 percent of organic samples still tested positive.

23
00:03:48,733 --> 00:04:04,096
A meta-analysis of crop studies puts the gap at residues on 11 percent of organic samples against 46 percent of conventional
ones, and an older analysis of American testing programmes found the amounts on organic samples consistently lower too.

24
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:08,963
Residues on food are one thing, though.
What actually ends up in your body?

25
00:04:09,467 --> 00:04:17,642
NutritionFacts.org says you can "turn on and off urinary
concentrations of pesticide metabolites like a light switch".

26
00:04:17,667 --> 00:04:31,842
That sounds like hype, but a systematic review backs it: all four trials it found that swapped people's whole diet to
organic and tested their urine, two in children and two in adults, found pesticide breakdown products fell significantly.

27
00:04:32,167 --> 00:04:37,967
The drop was often dramatic, as much as 90
percent, and it came within a few days.

28
00:04:37,967 --> 00:04:47,929
What these trials measure is exposure, not health, and a review of farming
systems notes that lower residues may not translate into better health outcomes.

29
00:04:48,433 --> 00:05:01,821
So yes: eating organic sharply and quickly lowers the pesticides on your food and in your body, but it does not
get rid of either entirely, and whether that makes you healthier is a separate question this evidence leaves open.

30
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:07,033
Put both answers together and the
organic label reads differently.

31
00:05:07,333 --> 00:05:15,646
The answers cover different ground, the field and your body, and
the slogan blurs them into a promise the rulebook never makes.

32
00:05:15,667 --> 00:05:23,579
So the next time someone tells you organic means no pesticides, ask the
better question: which ones, and how much of them ends up on your plate?
